<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Delaware River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Delaware River flows about 480 km from the Catskill Mountains to Delaware Bay, forming parts of the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> and Delaware. In the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, the Swedes of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the English competed for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> on its west bank in 1682. Washington's crossing of the river in December 1776 led to the American victory at Trenton.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delaware-river.jpg" alt="Delaware River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Famartin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2021-06-16_09_10_03_View_of_the_Delaware_Water_Gap_from_the_Delaware_River_Viaduct_over_the_Delaware_River_on_the_border_of_Knowlton_Township,_Warren_County,_New_Jersey_and_Upper_Mount_Bethel_Township,_Northampton_County,_Pennsylvania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
